@page "/components/accordion"

<PageTitle>Accordion — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">Accordion</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    One <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> per item, so it opens and closes with no JavaScript at all and
    survives a fragment swap without a compiler. Give every item the same <code>Name</code> and the
    browser closes the others for you — that is shadcn's <code>type="single"</code>, written as an
    attribute.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Basic</h2>
<Example Source="AccordionBasic.razor"><AccordionBasic /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Multiple</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Leave <code>Name</code> off. The browser only closes siblings that share a name, so
    <code>type="multiple"</code> here is the absence of an attribute rather than the presence of
    one.
</p>
<Example Source="AccordionMultiple.razor"><AccordionMultiple /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Disabled</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    A <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> has no disabled state — the element does not define one. Take
    it out of the tab order, stop the pointer, and say so with <code>aria-disabled</code>, which
    is the part a screen reader reads.
</p>
<Example Source="AccordionDisabled.razor"><AccordionDisabled /></Example>

